ines (n-2). So we could use a vertical line for column n-2, which is one of the non-sunny lines. So that part is fine.\n\nNow, after using a vertical line for column n-2, we have n-3 non-sunny lines left. The solution then says \"Analyzing columns x=n-1 (2 points) and x=n (1 point), we see that k=2 is impossible. The point (n,1) cannot be on a sunny line with points from column n-1.\" Let's examine this more generally. After using a vertical line for column n-2, we have n-3 non-sunny lines left. We need to cover columns n-1 and n. Column n-1 has two points: (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). Column n has one point: (n,1). The point (n,1) cannot be on a sunny line with any point from column n-1, as we saw. So to cover (n,1), we need either a non-sunny line (vertical x=n) or a sunny line that includes (n,1) and some point from earlier columns (like column n-2 or less). But we have already used a vertical line for column n-2, but we could still use a sunny line that includes (n,1) and some point from column n-2 (like (n-2,3) maybe). Let's check if (n,1) and (n-2,3) are collinear with a slope not 0, infinite, -1. Slope = (3-1)/(n-2-n) = 2/(-2) = -1, which is not allowed. So (n,1) and (n-2,3) are on a line of slope -1, which is non-sunny. So that doesn't help. (n,1) and (n-2,2) slope = (2-1)/(n-2-n) = 1/(-2) = -1/2, allowed. So (n,1) and (n-2,2) can be on a sunny line. But (n-2,2) is a point in column n-2, which is already covered by the vertical line x=n-2. However, we can still have a sunny line that also passes through (n-2,2). So we could use a sunny line to cover (n,1) and (n-2,2). Similarly, we could use a sunny line to cover (n-1,1) and (n-2,3) maybe? Let's check slope: (3-1)/(n-2-(n-1)) = 2/(-1) = -2, allowed. So (n-1,1) and (n-2,3) can be on a sunny line. And (n-1,2) and (n-2,1) slope = (1-2)/(n-2-(n-1)) = -1/(-1) = 1, allowed. So we could cover each of the three points (n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1) with three sunny lines each also covering a point from column n-2. But we have only k