e covered by a single sunny line. So we need a second sunny line that covers each individually, but a line can pass through a point and some other point already covered. For example, we could use a sunny line through (4,3) and (1,2) slope = (2-3)/(1-4) = -1/(-3) = 1/3, allowed. That line would also pass through (2,? ) maybe not integer. So we can use a sunny line L2: line through (4,3) and (1,2). That line also passes through (2,? ) maybe not integer. So L2 covers (4,3). Then we need to also cover (2,5). We could use a sunny line through (2,5) and (1,2) slope = (2-5)/(1-2) = -3/(-1) = 3, allowed. That line also passes through (3,? ) maybe not integer. So we could use a sunny line L3: line through (2,5) and (1,2). But we only have 2 sunny lines left, not 3. So we cannot cover both (4,3) and (2,5) with separate sunny lines if we already used one sunny line for (2,3)-(3,4). So we need to find a way to cover (4,3) and (2,5) with the same sunny line, but we saw that the line connecting them has slope -1, not allowed. So we cannot cover both with a single sunny line. So we need to adjust our earlier choices.\n\nPerhaps we could choose a different pairing for the first sunny line, maybe covering (4,3) and (2,5) is not allowed, but maybe we could cover (4,3) with a sunny line that also covers (2,5) indirectly via a third point? For example, a line through (4,3) and (1,6) slope = (6-3)/(1-4) = 3/(-3) = -1, not allowed. (4,3) and (3,5) not in set. (4,3) and (5,4) not in set. So (4,3) cannot be on a sunny line with any other point in the set except maybe (1,2) (slope 1/3) or (2,1) (slope (1-3)/(2-4) = -2/(-2) = 1, allowed). Actually (2,1) is in the set (b=1). So line through (4,3) and (2,1) slope = (1-3)/(2-4) = -2/(-2) = 1, allowed. So we could use a sunny line L2: y = x - 1 (since passes through (2,1) and (4,3)). That line also passes through (3,2) (which is covered by y=2) and (5,4) (not in set). So L2 covers (4,3). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover (2,5). Could we cover (2,5) 